INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(7494,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1913663','The contributions of managed care plans to public health practice: evidence from the nation''s largest local health departments.','Mays GP, Halverson PK, Stevens R.','2001','stange citrin commodity gorenflo gadow defriese hetherington kavaler dowd hmos dyal lisk grizzle starmer rolfs greenspan wasserheit kaluzny privatizing turnock potsic munson nalluri vaughn mattingly ludden hmos lumpkin landrum oldfield turnock kaluzny improv s1070 kaluzny kotelchuck kaluzny mckaig mckaig mullahy ado reconsidering retransformation econometrics s0167 bieler ennett zarkin 006x nudelman nejm199604183341611 roper koplan stinnett roper rundall schauffler koplan violante showstack leatherman inui mcafee handley puget hmos puget turnock turnock dyal vaughn rowitz munson balderson','Abstract OBJECTIVE: The authors examine the extent and nature of managed care plans participating in local public health activities. METHODS: In 1998, the authors surveyed the directors of all US local health departments serving jurisdictions of at least 100,000 residents to collect information about public health activities performed in their jurisdictions and about organizations participating in the activities. Multivariate logistic and linear regression models were used to examine organizational and market characteristics associated with managed care plan participation in public health activities. RESULTS: Managed care plans were reported to participate in public health activities in 164 (46%) of the jurisdictions surveyed, and to contribute to 13% of the public health activities performed in the average jurisdiction. Plans appeared most likely to participate in public health activities involving the delivery or management of personal health services and the exchange of health-related information. Managed care participation was more likely to occur in jurisdictions with higher HMO penetration, fewer competing plans, and larger proportions of plans enrolling Medicaid recipients. Participation was positively associated with the overall scope and perceived effectiveness of local public health activities. CONCLUSIONS: Although plans participate in a narrow range of activities, these contributions may complement the work of public health agencies. Full text PDF Previous 50 51 52 53 54 … 67 Next Selected References These references are in PubMed. This may not be the complete list of references from this article. Chapel T. J., Stange P. V., Gordon R. L., Miller A. Private sector health care organizations and essential public health services: potential effects on the practice of local public health. J Public Health Manag Pract. 1998 Jan;4(1):36–44. doi: 10.1097/00124784-199801000-00008. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Citrin T. Topics for our times: public health--community or commodity? Reflections on healthy communities. Am J Public Health. 1998 Mar;88(3):351–352. doi: 10.2105/ajph.88.3.351. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Corso L. C., Gorenflo G., Richards T. B., Taylor Z., Brown C. K., Gadow P. J. Assessing the impact of Medicaid managed care on TB activities in local health departments. J Public Health Manag Pract. 1998 Nov;4(6):62–68. doi: 10.1097/00124784-199811000-00010. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] DeFriese G. H., Hetherington J. S., Brooks E. F., Miller C. A., Jain S. C., Kavaler F., Stein J. S. The program implications of administrative relationships between local health departments and state and local government. Am J Public Health. 1981 Oct;71(10):1109–1115. doi: 10.2105/ajph.71.10.1109. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Dowd B. E. Financing preventive care in HMOs: a theoretical analysis. Inquiry. 1982 Spring;19(1):68–78. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Dyal W. W. Ten organizational practices of public health: a historical perspective. Am J Prev Med. 1995 Nov-Dec;11(6 Suppl):6–8. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Felt-L