cholar ] Turnock B. J., Handler A. S., Miller C. A. Core function-related local public health practice effectiveness. J Public Health Manag Pract. 1998 Sep;4(5):26–32. doi: 10.1097/00124784-199809000-00005. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Turnock B. J., Handler A., Dyal W. W., Christenson G., Vaughn E. H., Rowitz L., Munson J. W., Balderson T., Richards T. B. Implementing and assessing organizational practices in local health departments. Public Health Rep. 1994 Jul-Aug;109(4):478–484. [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Vogt R. L. Laboratory reporting and disease surveillance. J Public Health Manag Pract. 1996 Fall;2(4):28–30. doi: 10.1097/00124784-199623000-00008. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Wall S. Transformations in public health systems. Health Aff (Millwood) 1998 May-Jun;17(3):64–80. doi: 10.1377/hlthaff.17.3.64. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',2,'2025-10-20 07:26:27','2025-10-20 07:26:27');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(7495,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11603824','Impact of social determinants of health on rehabilitation service use and outcomes in adults in the USA: a scoping review protocol.','Wickstrom J, Leone EJ, Sasson N, Morris HC, Livinski AA, Camarillo ND, Lely J, Venkatarajan R, Goldberg J, Asante-Otoo A, Rasch EK.','2024','listservs worship neighbourhood unjustly ideologies unrecognised summarising collate neighbourhood polluted 4note discerned discerned sdohsocial generalisable lely abided aal proquest emtree prescreened prescreened prescreening apta asha aota atra acrm naric veritas voters voter prescreen snowballing prescreening apta asha aota atra acrm naric provo extractors _________ elementdata neighbourhood tables2 sdohsocial listservs aal prepublication wickstrom jwickstrom lifebridgehealth eleone lifebridgehealth noa sasson noa sasson hailey haileycmorris1 alicia livinski alicia livinski camarillo ncamaril justine lely justine lely rajpriya venkatarajan rvenkatarajan lifebridgehealth afua asante otoo afua asante rasche enlace feuerstein fullard thibault ecronicon oakes hoenig atchison tb02131 healthypeople pronk s249 jphr verduzco molinares odonkor esparza costigan austerity chastain jamda costigan keitt irvin otjr sayner toohey pzac106 pzac106 linguistically mandalia parzick jse easter jvoice frier braaten hanebuth mdj piovesana lely sasson fram lillie aromataris synthesismanual polanin pigott espelage synth jrsm','Abstract Abstract Introduction Many Americans fail to receive equitable access to or positive outcomes from rehabilitation services. These disparities result from broader societal inequities that disproportionately affect certain groups of people due to non-medical factors such as race, gender, education and living status. This scoping review aims to describe research papers that examine social determinants of health (SDOH) that underlie disparities in access to, use of, or outcomes related to rehabilitation (in occupational therapy, physical therapy, recreational therapy, rehabilitation medicine/physiatry and speech-language pathology) among adults in the USA. Identifying SDOH-related factors that drive inequities in rehabilitation will inform the development of potential solutions to improve access, professional education and patient outcomes. Methods and analysis The Joanna Briggs Institute methodological framework will be used to conduct this scoping review. To meet the inclusion criteria, articles will need to be published in English since 2013, be classified as full-length original research, include at least 100 sampled adults, be conducted in the USA or its territories, and investigate SDOH-related disparities in rehabilitation. Search terms will include SDOH, rehabilitation and disparities. Five databases (Embase, PubMed, Scopus, Sociological Abstracts and Web of Science: Core Collection) will be searched for articles published in English between 2013 and 2023. Records from these databases as well as reference lists of included papers and review papers will be screened for eligibility. Two people will independently review each record during title and abstract screening and full-text screening, and three people will independently extract data. Screening and data extraction processes will be piloted. The results will be presented in tables, graphs and narrative summaries. Ethics and dissemination Findings will be disseminated through scientific conferences, professional networks, stakeholder meetings, invited workshops and webinars, email listservs of professional associations, academic social media and in a peer-reviewed journal. Ethics approval is not required as the review will only include previously published data. Keywords: Health Equity, REHABILITATION MEDICINE, Review, Health Services Accessibility, Research Design STRENGTHS AND LIMITATIONS OF THIS STUDY. The focus of this review includes a comprehensive search of the literature regarding social determinants of health explored across a vast array of rehabilitation settings (inpatient, outpatient, home) in the following disciplines: occupational therapy, physical therapy, recreational therapy, rehabilitation medicine/physiatry and speech-language pathology. The search will include records pulled from five databases (Embase, PubMed, Scopus, Sociological Abstracts and Web of Science: Core Collection) as well as articles identified from screening the reference lists of included studies and relevant review papers. The search terms were extremely challenging to develop, as various terms are used by authors to describe ‘disparities’ and ‘social determinants of health’; despite an iterative process to establish our search strategy with our biomedical librarian, it is possible that our review will not include the full breadth of the literature on our topic due to our search term selection. Stakeholders will be involved in the conduct of this review to ensure that the findings are supported by their experiences and to assist with directing future research priorities; stakeholders will meet with us to discuss and interpret the results as well as assist with identifying funding priorities based on research gaps we identify on this topic. This review will be limited to publications in the English language only, since that is the primary language spoken in the USA, but it is possible we may miss relevant studies accordingly. Introduction Background and rationale With its shifting age structure and ever-growing population, the USA is experiencing a rise in chronic conditions, illnesses and injuries, ultimately leading to a steep demand for rehabilitation services. 1 However, despite global 2 efforts to address this problem, a surprisingly small number of Americans in need of services are able to receive them; 3 4 among those who do receive services, many fail to achieve favourable outcomes. For example, racial-ethnic minority groups are less likely to receive postacute rehabilitation after stroke, traumatic brain injury, hip fracture and spinal cord injury. 5 Black adults with traumatic brain injury have been shown to have lower functional ability at discharge from rehabilitation compared with non-Hispanic white adults. 6 These disparities are inequitable, directly related to the historical and current unequal distribution of resources, and disproportionately affect certain groups of people (eg, factors such as race, gender, education and living status). 5 Identifying the social and economic factors contributing to health disparities in rehabilitation is an essential step in navigating the barriers preventing Americans from achieving optimal health and functioning. This mission aligns with the priority areas identified by Healthy People 2030, an initiative striving to address the social determinants of health (SDOH) in the USA to facilitate advancements in health equity and health literacy. 7 This initiative defines SDOH as, ‘the conditions in the environments where people are born, live, learn, work, play, worship, and age that affect a wide range of health, functioning, and quality-of-life outcomes and risks,’ which are organised into five domains: economic stability, education access and quality, healthcare access and quality, neighbourhood and built environment and social and community context. 7 SDOH are associated with opportunities and resources that maintain or improve one’s health, and they have a major influence on general well-being. 8 Health inequities driven by SDOH occur when underlying social structures and systemic processes unjustly assign people to different social positions. These opaque yet impactful allocation processes evolve over time and are reinforced by social ideologies and practice, 9 resulting in unintentional or unrecognised disparities. Thus, for Americans in need of rehabilitation services, it is essential to examine the SDOH-related barriers contributing to disparate access to, use of, or outcomes related to rehabilitation services. Several review papers have been published in recent years regarding SDOH in the context of rehabilitation; however, these reviews have generally had a narrow focus on a single SDOH category (race/ethnicity 10 11 and non-urban settings), 12 , 14 condition (anterior cruciate ligament injury, 15 intellectual and developmental disabilities, 16 pelvic floor issues, 17 persistent pain, 18 rotator cuff repairs, 19 voice and upper airway disorders 20 and neurological conditions) 21 or rehabilitation discipline (occupational therapy, 12 14 physical therapy 15 18 22 and speech-language pathology). 20 While the literature has described SDOH as they broadly relate to general health outcomes in the US healthcare system, it fails to comprehensively or systematically describe the complex, multifactorial interplay between SDOH and rehabilitation services. The current scoping review aims to explore this gap by summarising SDOH-related disparities examined in relation to rehabilitation access, use and outcomes. With a broader scope than the aforementioned reviews, this review will include all SDOH categories and conditions examined across a variety of rehabilitation disciplines: occupational therapy, physical therapy, recreational therapy, rehabilitation medicine/physiatry and speech-language pathology. Rehabilitation settings considered in this review will also be all-encompassing, spanning from acute hospital care to postacute inpatient rehabilitation, home health, telehealth and outpatient care. We chose to conduct a scoping review rather than another method of data synthesis because scoping reviews are designed to identify and map the available evidence regarding a broad topic and its related concepts. 23 Thus, in alignment with our primary objective, a scoping review is appropriate because we aim to investigate a broad, exploratory question with expansive inclusion criteria and provide a clear indication of the volume of literature on this topic. 23 Since our objective is to characterise the literature on SDOH in the context of rehabilitation, a scoping review will help us better understand what research has been done, recognise the current gaps in knowledge and identify subsequent steps to address inequities. As the number of people requiring rehabilitation services continues to rise, it is crucial to expand our understanding of the intricate and dynamic relationships between SDOH and rehabilitation services. This scoping review will allow us to identify these relationships using the Healthy People 2030 framework, 7 the methods used to explore them (eg, study designs, data sources and study location), and the samples in which they have been examined (eg, sample size, race/ethnicity, sex/gender, conditions and age). Improved insight into such nuanced relationships will allow for the development of innovative solutions that address the factors contributing to rehabilitation-related disparities and will inform the education of key stakeholders, collaboration with public health organisations and creation of equitable health policy and practice. Objective The aim of this scoping review is to collate and describe research papers published since 2013 that investigate SDOH-related disparities in access to, use of, or outcomes related to rehabilitation. Specifically, the relationship between SDOH and rehabilitation—in the context of occupational therapy, physical therapy, recreational therapy, rehabilitation medicine/physiatry and speech-language pathology—will be investigated in adults in the USA. Methods and analysis Planning for this project began in October 2023, and the expected end date (journal submission) is January 2025. Eligibility criteria We drafted eligibility criteria ( table 1 ) for title and abstract screening and full-text screening through an iterative process to establish a shared understanding and consistent application of criteria among reviewers. Any modifications made to these criteria during screening will be documented in the final scoping review. Table 1. Proposed eligibility criteria. Inclusion criteria (all must be true to pass) Exclusion criteria (at least one must be true to fail) Article passes criteria if: Article fails criteria if: 1. Written in English 1. Not written in English 2. Published 2013 or later 2. Published prior to 2013 3. Includes at least one SDOH (as defined by Healthy People 2030 Healthy People 2030), 7 or a synonym thereof. Domains (examples) include: Economic stability (poverty, low SES/income, job opportunities/employment, food insecurity and housing instability/homelessness) Education access and quality (early childhood development/education, higher education, high school graduation and literacy and language skills) Healthcare access and quality (access to health services/primary care, geographic barriers/rural/urban, insurance coverage and health literacy/communication) Neighbourhood and built environment (safe/quality housing, crime and violence exposure, polluted water/air, transportation access, food access and physical activity access) Social and community context (civic participation, racism, gender, discrimination, incarceration and social cohesion/support/networks) 3. Does not include at least one SDOH listed in inclusion criterion #3 4. Mentions at least one of the following rehabilitation disciplines: Inpatient rehabilitation Occupational therapy Physiatry Physical medicine and rehabilitation (PM&R) Physical therapy Recreational therapy Speech-language pathology 4. Does not mention at least one of the rehabilitation disciplines listed in inclusion criterion #4Note: fail article if ONLY about general ‘rehabilitation’ or ‘physical rehabilitation’ or ‘outpatient therapy’ 5. Examines SDOH-related disparities in access to, use of, or outcomes related to rehabilitation 5. Does not examine SDOH-related disparities in access to, use of, or outcomes related to rehabilitation 6. Publication type is identified as full-length original research 6. Publication type is not identified as full-length original research, such as one of the following: Brief reports/case studies Comment/correspondence/letter to editor Conference abstract/proceeding/series Consensus statement Editorial/opinion/perspective Literature search meta-analysis/review Position statement Pilot/preliminary study Study protocol 7. Study is conducted only in the states or territories 31 of the USA.Note: during title and abstract screening, if geographical location cannot be discerned but article meets all other inclusion criteria, pass on to full-text screening 7. Study is not conducted in the USA or its territories OR is conducted in the USA or its territories in addition to other countries 8. Participant sample: Only includes humans in vivo AND Contains adults (18+ years) AND Contains at least 100 people AND Has tried (or been referred) to access, has used, or has outcomes related to rehabilitation Note: during title and abstract screening, if sample information cannot be discerned but article meets all other inclusion criteria, pass on to full-text screening 8. Participant sample: Does not include humans in vivo OR Does not include adults OR Is labelled as paediatric/adolescent/transition-age youth (even if 18+) OR Includes animals, cadavers or humans in vitro OR Does not include patients (focuses on the education/knowledge/competency of providers, trainees or students) OR Contains fewer than 100 people 9. Results: Are provided for disparities/differences between SDOH-related groups AND Are provided for at least one of the rehabilitation disciplines listed in inclusion criterion #4 AND Show a direct relationship between SDOH and rehabilitation 9. Results: Are not provided for disparities/differences between SDOH-related groups OR Are not provided for at least one of the rehabilitation disciplines listed in inclusion criterion #4 OR Do not show a direct relationship between SDOH and rehabilitation Open in a new tab SDOHsocial determinants of health Participants Studies that include adult participants (ages 18 years and older) who have tried to access or have used rehabilitation services will be included. A sample size of at least 100 will be required to ensure sufficient power (generalisable results). 24 Concept Sources must examine SDOH, rehabilitation and disparities. Specifically, each source must include (1) at least one SDOH (as defined by Healthy People 2030 Healthy People 2030), 7 (2) at least one rehabilitation discipline: occupational therapy, physical therapy, recreational therapy, rehabilitation medicine/physiatry and speech-language pathology and (3) an SDOH-related disparity in access to, use of, or outcomes related to rehabilitation. Context This review will only consider studies that have been conducted in the USA and/or its territories due to vast global differences in the structure of healthcare and health policies. Studies conducted outside of (or in addition to) the USA and/or its territories will be excluded. We will limit to articles published in English since it is the predominant language in our setting of interest. Types of sources Only full-length original research articles will be considered for inclusion. Examples of publication types that will be excluded can be found in table 1 . Articles must be published since 2013 to capture the most recent literature on this topic. Search strategy To write this protocol, we followed guidance from Lely and colleagues (2023) 25 and abided by the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses extension for Scoping Reviews (PRISMA-ScR). 26 The proposed scoping review will be conducted in accordance with the Joanna Briggs Institute methodology for scoping reviews 27 and reported using the PRISMA-ScR. 26 The results of the search will be reported in full in the final scoping review and presented in a PRISMA flow diagram. 28 The comprehensive search strategy will be developed by a biomedical librarian (AAL) through iterative consultation with the review team. The final strategy will be validated through the retrieval of a key set of relevant records. The databases (vendors) to be searched are Embase (Elsevier), PubMed (US National Library of Medicine), Scopus (Elsevier), Sociological Abstracts (ProQuest) and Web of Science: Core Collection (Clarivate Analytics). The searches will be limited by publication year (January 2013–December 2023), language (English) and type (full-length original research). This will allow us to capture the most recent literature published in the predominant language of our region of interest. Controlled vocabulary terms (eg, MeSH, EMTREE) and keywords to be used for the main concepts of our research question include SDOH, rehabilitation and disparities. See online supplemental appendix A for the anticipated full search strategy for one database (PubMed). In addition to searching databases, the reference lists of (1) included records (articles that passed full-text review) and (2) relevant review papers (systematic, scoping, meta-analysis, narrative) will be prescreened to identify other potentially relevant articles. To identify the review papers, a separate search will be conducted in PubMed using a modified version of the original search strategy. Resulting review papers will be prescreened for eligibility (to ensure the review papers are relevant to our topic). For the review papers that pass this prescreening phase, the reference lists of each review paper will be inspected for potential relevance. References that pass this initial inspection will then be uploaded to Covidence, and similar to papers identified by the main search strategy, these records will go through title and abstract screening and full-text screening (as appropriate). Professional rehabilitation association websites will also be searched to identify any non-indexed grey literature (see details in ‘Study Selection’ for how this will be done). The websites to be searched include: American Physical Therapy Association (APTA), American Speech-Language-Hearing Association (ASHA), American Occupational Therapy Association (AOTA), Association of Academic Physiatrists (AAP), American Academy of Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation (AAPM&R), American Therapeutic Recreation Association (ATRA), American Congress of Rehabilitation Medicine (ACRM) and National Rehabilitation Information Centre (NARIC). Study selection Following the searches, all records will be collated and imported into EndNote V.20 (Clarivate Analytics, Philadelphia, Pennsylvania, USA), and duplicates will be identified and removed. The unique records from the literature searches will be exported into Covidence (Veritas Health Innovation, Melbourne, Australia) for screening, and any newly identified duplicate records will be removed. Articles will be screened using Covidence in two phases: title and abstract screening and full-text screening. For title and abstract screening, the process will be piloted until 80% voting agreement is achieved across all voters (not just within voter pairs); the first pilot round will include approximately 20 records, and subsequent rounds will consist of 10 records each until the threshold is reached. A screening schedule will be implemented based on the total number of search results; the schedule will include a list of articles, due dates and meeting dates to discuss discrepancies. Two independent votes will be required per record, and voting discrepancies will be resolved by a third reviewer. If a record does not include an abstract (ie, title only), it will be passed onto the full-text stage. For full-text screening, records that pass title and abstract screening will have the corresponding full-texts uploaded into Covidence. All team members will complete an iterative piloting process for full-text screening until a 90% voting agreement is attained. A voting and meeting schedule will be implemented. Two team members will vote on each record independently, and discrepancies will be resolved through consensus with a third reviewer. Reasons for exclusion will be recorded and reported in the final scoping review. One team member will independently prescreen the reference lists of (1) records included after full-text screening and (2) review articles (systematic, scoping, meta-analysis and narrative) related to our topic. Reference snowballing will not be conducted. Records will be excluded if published prior to 2013 or if the title is not written in English, only mentions children, lists a country other than or in addition to the USA, does not mention an SDOH or a rehabilitation discipline/disparity or clearly indicates a publication type that is not original research. All records that are selected during reference list prescreening will be uploaded into Covidence and will go through the same screening process as records retrieved from the literature databases, as detailed in the previous paragraph. One team member conducted a preliminary search of 24 websites representing government agencies, non-profit organisations and professional rehabilitation associations to identify which websites might contain relevant evidence that addresses our research question. It was determined that the websites for professional organisations provided the best yield of relevant information and will form the basis for the grey literature search. One team member will search eight professional websites (APTA, ASHA, AOTA, AAP, AAPM&R, ATRA, ACRM and NARIC). The team member will independently search using the websites’ native search feature for records published since 2013 using various combinations of terms including SDOH, rehabilitation and health disparities. Records that appear relevant will be uploaded into Covidence and will go through full-text screening. Data charting A data extraction form will be created by the data analyst (ie, first author) in Qualtrics XM (Qualtrics, Provo, Utah, USA); a draft of this form is provided in online supplemental appendix B . Repeated pilots of the form will be conducted on up to 5 articles per round until extractors and senior team members have a shared understanding of elements to extract (80% agreement). Full-text articles for the pilot will be selected at random by the data analyst. The data extraction form will be modified with each pilot round based on extraction discrepancies and feedback from team members. After piloting is complete, a weekly extraction schedule will be developed based on the total number of included articles; this schedule will include a list of assigned articles, due dates and meeting dates to discuss discrepancies. Three team members will independently extract information from each record in accordance with the schedule; discrepancies will be reviewed and resolved in the presence of the data analyst during weekly meetings by referring to the original text and reaching consensus through discussion. The following data will be extracted: study objectives, SDOH—rehabilitation discipline relationships (eg, insurance status—physical therapy), data source information (eg, Burn Model Systems), geographical location (eg, Nebraska), rehabilitation setting (eg, inpatient care), sample race/ethnicity, sample sex/gender, group label (condition or description), sample size ( n ), sample age in years (mean, SD, median, IQR). These extracted data are subject to change based on included sources and the ongoing process of screening; modifications will be detailed in the final scoping review. Rules regarding data extraction will be documented directly in the data extraction form. There are no plans to contact the authors of articles to obtain missing or unclear information; instead, this information will be coded accordingly. Supplemental files of all articles will be checked for relevant information associated with our data elements. In cases in which articles refer to previous publications for information, we will not check those referenced publications and will indicate that the information is ‘Not Reported’ in the extraction form. If multiple articles from the same single study are identified, we will follow the Cochrane Handbook for Systematic Reviews of Interventions (chapter 5) guidance. 29 Data analysis and presentation After data extraction is complete, a quality assurance check will be conducted for all extracted data. Specifically, each data element will be checked by a team member for accuracy by comparing the extracted data against the original full-text article using our final agreed-on rules (documented in the extraction form). This process will help ensure that our extracted data is accurate and is not affected by coder drift or misunderstanding. 30 After the quality assurance check, data will be cleaned and coded by the data analyst in Microsoft Excel (Microsoft Corporation, Redmond, Washington, USA). Data elements with strictly closed-ended responses will not require any coding (ie, data source, rehabilitation setting) unless they contain write-in categories, such as ‘other: _________’ (ie, geographical location, race/ethnicity, sex/gender), in which case the extracted text will need to be grouped into its own categories or combined with already existing ones. Data elements with numerical data entry (ie, sample size, age) will not require coding either. For open-ended response data elements, formatting changes will be necessary to achieve a consistent appearance and improve readability (ie, study objectives, SDOH—rehabilitation discipline; group labels) and/or will need categorised into appropriate subgroups (ie, SDOH—rehabilitation discipline, geographical location; see table 2 for potential subgroups). Table 2. Descriptive summary of data elements. Data elementData element subgroups Number of articles (n) Percent of articles (%) SDOH domain Economic stability Education access and quality Healthcare access and quality Neighbourhood and built environment Social and community context Rehabilitation discipline Occupational therapy Physical therapy Recreational therapy Rehabilitation medicine/physiatry Speech-language pathology Study location West Midwest South Northeast Territories Not Reported Rehabilitation setting Inpatient care Outpatient care In-home care Telehealth Not reported Data source category Clinical trial Database (claims data) Database (model systems) Database (state, regional or national) Medical record review Race/ethnic subgroups American Indian/Alaska Native Asian Black/African American Hispanic or Latino Native Hawaiian/Other Pacific Islander White Other (may be further specified) Not reported Sex/gender subgroups Females Males Other (may be further specified) Open in a new tab SDOH, social determinants of health Given the primary aims of this scoping review, descriptive statistics (primarily frequency counts and percentages) will be used to summarise findings for the data elements. Data analysis will be conducted using R V.4.3.2 (The R Foundation, Vienna, Austria). Draft tables included here (see tables2 4 ) demonstrate how we intend to report our findings; however, it is possible that our processes for data synthesis and presenting results may change as we advance through the review process and become more familiar with the structure of included articles. Any modifications to these processes will be reported in the final scoping review. Narrative summaries will accompany tables and figures to describe results. Table 4. Description of each article’s objectives and SDOH–rehabilitation relationships. Article reference Study objective(s) SDOH category Access/use of/outcomes related to rehabilitation Rehabilitation category Reference 1 Reference 2 Open in a new tab SDOHsocial determinants of health Table 3. Descriptive summary of study sample-related data elements. Article reference Sample group Group label (condition/description) Sample size (n) Age in years(mean ± SD or median (IQR)) Reference 1 1 2 Overall Reference 2 1 2 3 4 Overall Open in a new tab Ethics and dissemination Ethics Ethical approval is not required for this scoping review as it will only include data from previously published papers. Dissemination Findings from this scoping review will be disseminated through scientific conferences, professional networks, stakeholder meetings, invited workshops and webinars, email listservs of professional associations, academic social media and in a peer-reviewed journal. Patient and public involvement Patients or the public were not included in the planning of this scoping review (ie, writing this protocol), but stakeholders will be involved throughout the course of this review to enhance the usefulness/application of the findings of our scoping review publication. Specifically, they will assist with interpreting the results, disseminating the findings and identifying funding priorities to include in the final scoping review. supplementary material online supplemental file 1 bmjopen-14-11-s001.pdf (67.8KB, pdf) DOI: 10.1136/bmjopen-2024-087254 online supplemental file 2 bmjopen-14-11-s002.pdf (134.3KB, pdf) DOI: 10.1136/bmjopen-2024-087254 Footnotes Funding: This work was supported by the Intramural Research Program (IRP) of the National Institutes of Health (NIH), Clinical Center, and AAL’s work was in support of the NIH IRP as part of the NIH Library, Office of Research Services. Prepublication history and additional supplemental material for this paper are available online. To view these files, please visit the journal online ( https://doi.org/10.1136/bmjopen-2024-087254 ). Provenance and peer review: Not commissioned; externally peer reviewed. Patient consent for publication: Not applicable. Patient and public involvement: Patients and/or the public were not involved in the design, conduct, reporting or dissemination plans of this research. Contributor Information Jordan Wickstrom, Email: jwickstrom@lifebridgehealth.org. Emma J Leone, Email: eleone@lifebridgehealth.org. Noa Sasson, Email