INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(7505,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9987327','Randomized controlled trial of suprascapular nerve blocks for subacute adhesive capsulitis.','Schiltz M, Beeckmans N, Gillard B, DE Baere T, Hatem SM.','2022','suprascapular suprascapular suprascapular suprascapular ropivacaine suprascapular murley murley suprascapular suprascapular rediscussed bursitis ropivacaine gleno humeral dupuytren suprascapular innervates acromio clavicular wassef suprascapular ssnb ssnb ssnb ssnb ssnb ropivacaine murley clingov radiculopathy gleno humeral suprascapular ropivacaine undistinguishable ropivacaine suprascapular ropivacaine suprascapular suprascapular spinoglenoid fujifilm sonosite bothell hfl 50x 6mhz scapular suprascapular konix turkuaz suprascapular suprascapular murley murley murley mcid mcid goniometer sabari imaginable foregoing 1sd demographical ropivacaine demographical painkillers murley ropivacaine suprascapular murley murley murley murley groupxtime mcid groupxtime ropivacaine ssnb murley ropivacaine suprascapular murley ropivacaine ssnb suprascapular suprascapular ssnbs ssnb ssnbs ssnbs ssnb ssnb zygoapophysary feigl supraspinous suprascapular ropivacaine peroneal ropivacaine ssnbs ssnb gorthi ssnb ssnbs unblinding suprascapular suprascapular ssnbs ssnbs rediscussed ssnb neviaser hannafin clipsham jse schiefer fontenelle carminatti righi jse devaraj bunker dupuytren maund suekarran neilson brealey hta16110 bou haidar cutts massoud klc filiz cakr toraman suprascapular dahan vadeboncoeur suissa bupivacaine suprascapular wassef suprascapular tb02007 neyaz handa suprascapular injr injr_11_19 ozkan ozcekic sarar cift ozkan unay suprascapular 354x suprascapular suprascapular e20987 karatas meray suprascapular taskaynatan ozgul yazicioglu kalyon suprascapular tohoku tjem teja dram banshiwal drrc intraarticular suprascapular ortho i2f umphrey smuck fredericson shanahan ahern wetherall bresnihan suprascapular bupivacaine suprascapular mortada ezzeldin ammar salama suprascapular bmr chattopadhyay suprascapular saglam alisar suprascapular murley neviaser neviaser cios goniometric bærheim kvale intertester s12891 bellingham suprascapular hearty suprascapular macdermid woodhouse murley jse devji zeraatkar qasim siemieniuk e028777 sabari maltzev lubarsky liszkay homel goniometric candela giannicola passaretti venditto gumina s12306 mokrysz nosek nrn3475 lho serna ledesma alayon pruimboom mystery dakin rangan brealey northgraves kottam kez007 colloca pharmtox colloca barsky nocebo nejmra1907805 finniss brooker e744 pr9 previtali merli frattura candrian zaffagnini filardo 185s 96s saltzman leroux basques chahal feigl anderhuber dorn pipam rosmarin likar suprascapular gadsden rothe lundstrøm ropivacaine peroneal mølleskov rothe lundstrøm ropivacaine peroneal suprascapular gorthi suprascapular perishoulder suprascapular 0b013e3182204ec0 mcbain cd011275 cd011275','Abstract BACKGROUND Shoulder pain and loss of function remain a therapeutic challenge in adhesive capsulitis. Suprascapular nerve blocks, a common treatment in adhesive capsulitis, are considered a safe and effective method for the resolution of pain and restoration of shoulder range of motion (ROM). To our knowledge, no data are available on the use of suprascapular nerve blocks in adhesive capsulitis in the subacute phase. AIM The aim of this study was to compare the efficacy of ultrasound-guided suprascapular nerve blocks versus saline injections for treating adhesive capsulitis in the subacute phase. DESIGN Randomized double-blinded controlled trial; level of evidence 2. SETTING Out-patient consultation of Physical and Rehabilitation Medicine in a general hospital. POPULATION Thirty-five patients with subacute adhesive capsulitis. METHODS Patients were randomly allocated to receive either 3 successive (1-week interval) ultrasound-guided suprascapular nerve blocks with ropivacaine 5 mL 2 mg/mL (intervention group) or ultrasound-guided injections of 5 mL sterile saline solution (NaCl 0.9%) (control group), at the floor of the suprascapular fossa. Primary outcome was shoulder function assessed by the Constant-Murley Score. Secondary outcomes were shoulder ROM and shoulder pain intensity. Assessments were performed before each injection and 4 weeks after the last injection. RESULTS A significant increase of Constant-Murley Score (P<0.001), increase of shoulder ROM (all directions: P<0.011) and decrease of pain (P<0.001), were observed over time in both study groups. However, no significant differences were observed between the intervention and the control group. CONCLUSIONS Three successive suprascapular nerve blocks did not provide a better outcome than saline injections on shoulder function, ROM, and pain in subacute adhesive capsulitis. These negative findings warrant some considerations on the natural history of adhesive capsulitis, as well as timing, type, and placebo effects of injections. CLINICAL REHABILITATION IMPACT The current place of suprascapular nerve blocks in the treatment strategy of adhesive capsulitis needs to be rediscussed. K ey words : Bursitis, Shoulder, Nerve block, Placebos, Ropivacaine, Saline solution Adhesive capsulitis (or frozen shoulder) is characterized by spontaneous onset shoulder pain and restriction of active and passive range of motion (ROM) of the gleno-humeral joint. It is a common problem in a general musculoskeletal setting such as orthopedic surgery, rheumatology and physical medicine and rehabilitation consultations. The incidence of adhesive capsulitis is estimated at 2-5% in the general population. 1, 2 In most cases, there is no identified etiology (primary adhesive capsulitis). Secondary adhesive capsulitis may be due to trauma, surgical interventions, diabetes mellitus, thyroid dysfunction, Dupuytren’s disease, auto-immune disorders, breast cancer treatment, cerebrovascular accident, or myocardial infarction. 1, 3-5 The clinical evolution of adhesive capsulitis is typically characterized by four overlapping phases 6 and treatment should be tailored to the stages of the disease. 7 At present, no therapeutic intervention is universally accepted as most effective for restoring motion and alleviating pain in this population. 1 During the initial and most painful freezing stages, the most important goal of treatment is to decrease the debilitating pain. 7 The suprascapular nerve innervates 70% of the shoulder area, including the superior and posterior regions of the shoulder joint, the capsule and the acromio-clavicular joint. 8, 9 In 1992, Wassef 10 first described a suprascapular nerve block (SSNB) for frozen shoulder. Many previous studies have shown that SSNB is safe and effective in primary and secondary adhesive capsulitis. 8, 11-20 Nevertheless, the overall quality of studies was moderate with study designs not being randomized, 10, 12, 13, 18 or insufficiently blinded. 8, 10, 12, 13, 17, 18, 21, 22 Furthermore, previous studies typically recruited patients with chronic capsulitis (longer than 6 months) 8, 9, 13, 15-18 or used a combination of SSNB and glucocorticoid injections. 8, 12, 13, 17, 19, 22, 23 There appears to be a lack of information on the effectiveness of SSNB as in patients with adhesive capsulitis at the subacute stage. The aim of this prospective randomized double-blinded placebo-controlled trial was to evaluate the effectiveness of repeated ultrasound-guided SSNB with 5 mL of ropivacaine 2 mg/mL versus saline injections in subacute adhesive capsulitis. The primary outcome was shoulder function assessed with the Constant-Murley Score (CMS). 24 Secondary outcomes were shoulder joint range of motion (ROM) and pain intensity on a Visual Analogue Scale (VAS). It was hypothesized that patients in the intervention group would have increased shoulder function and ROM, and decreased pain scores compared to patients in the control group. Materials and methods Participants This study was designed as a prospective, randomized, double-blinded, placebo-controlled trial (CLINGOV registry number: 2016-10/02). It was approved by the institutional research ethics committee (Ethics committee Clinique St-Jean, Brussels, Belgium; chairperson: Dr R. Leach; protocol number: 2016 -10/04; date of approval: 18/11/2016) before the trial was started and has been conducted in accordance with the principles set forth in the Code of Ethics of the World Medical Association (Declaration of Helsinki). Patients eligible for this study were recruited from the out-patient consultation of the Department of Physical Medicine and Orthopedics of a general hospital (Clinique St-Jean, Brussels, Belgium) from September 2016 to May 2020. Inclusion criteria were age >18 years, presence of unilateral or bilateral adhesive capsulitis of the shoulder, duration of symptoms between 2 weeks and 6 months. Exclusion criteria were history of trauma at the onset of symptoms, rheumatologic disease, neurologic disease, cervical radiculopathy, coagulation disorder, hypersensitivity to local anesthetics, inability to understand the study procedure, or pregnancy. The diagnosis of adhesive capsulitis was made by an experienced physician (M.S.) based on clinical history and detailed physical and neurological examination. 25 Clinical criteria for diagnosing adhesive capsulitis were pain and stiffness in a shoulder combined with ROM that was significantly restricted in all active and passive movements of the gleno-humeral joint. 26-28 If necessary, imaging studies were performed to exclude other causes of shoulder pain and control for exclusion criteria. Patients who were eligible for this study based on the inclusion and exclusion criteria, received information on the study procedure by the principal investigator (M.S.) and were included after providing written informed consent. Patients were instructed to continue with their habitual medication and physical therapy during the study, as prescribed before inclusion. Procedures All patients received three injections in the shoulder region: either a suprascapular nerve block with ropivacaine (intervention group) or saline injections (control group). Patients were evaluated at 4 time points: before the first injection (T0), before the second injection (T1), before the third injection (T2), and one month after the third injection (T3). Injections at T0, T1 and T2 were scheduled at 1-week intervals. Using a statistical random number generator, included patients were randomly allocated to the intervention group or placebo group. This randomization was organized by an experienced hospital pharmacist who provided undistinguishable 5 mL-syringes filled with ropivacaine or saline solution, with individual patient’s labels. All injections were performed by the same blinded, board-certified PMR specialist (M.S.). Patients of the intervention group received ultrasound-guided suprascapular nerve blocks with 5 mL of ropivacaine 2 mg/mL. Patients of the control group received ultrasound-guided injections of 5 mL sterile saline solution (NaCl 0.9%). The fluid was injected at the floor of the suprascapular fossa, between the suprascapular notch and the spinoglenoid notch, as described by Peng et al . 29 and Harmon et al . 30 The patient was placed in a sitting position with the study side hand resting on the contralateral leg. To obtain an uninterrupted view of the ultrasound screen, the operator positioned himself behind the patient with the ultrasound machine (M-TURBO ® from Fujifilm SonoSite Inc, Bothell, WA, USA) in front of the patient. An initial scan was performed with the ultrasound transducer (HFL 50X/15-6MHz, 38 mm broadband linear array) parallel to the scapular spine to identify the suprascapular fossa. The ultrasound transducer was then inserted into a sterile protection sheath, the skin was cleaned with a chlorhexidine solution. A sterile ultrasound gel (Konix ® sterile contact medium for ultrasonic and electrical transmission gel from Turkuaz Inc, Istanbul, Turkey) was used to perform the procedure. Under aseptic conditions, a 21-Gauge, 50-mm needle was inserted (with an in-plane approach) in the longitudinal axis of the ultrasound beam to localize the tip of the needle near to the suprascapular nerve in the suprascapular notch. After an aspiration test to double-check needle positioning because of the nearby artery, 5 mL of fluid was injected with the spread being visualized. Assessments Patients were assessed for shoulder function with the Constant-Murley Score, for shoulder range of motion and for shoulder pain intensity. Evaluations were performed by two PMR specialists who were blinded for the randomization and trained for using the Constant-Murley Score and the visual analogue pain scale. The same patient was always evaluated by the same physician to minimize inter-investigator variability. The functional status of the shoulder was assessed with the Constant-Murley Score. 24, 31 The CMS is a composite scale divided into four sections: pain (15 points), activities of daily living (20 points), ROM (40 points) and strength (25 points) with a total score ranging from 0 (worst possible) to 100 (best possible). Patients were asked to verbally rate each parameter. Patients were categorized as responders or non-responders to treatment as a function of their change in CMS score (T3 minus T0). The median estimate for minimal clinical important difference (MCID) for the CMS is 8.3 according to Hao et al . 32 Thus, patients with a change of CMS of more than 8.3 were considered as responders. The number of patients reaching the MCID was calculated in each group. Active and passive ROM in anterior elevation and abduction were measured using an adapted (large, total length 50 cm) goniometer, as described by Riddle et al . and Sabari et al ., 27, 28, 33 and values were expressed in degrees °. External and internal rotation were scored according to their specific scale in the CMS – ROM section. 24, 31 The intensity of shoulder pain was assessed with a numerical Visual Analogue Scale (from 0 to 10 with “0” corresponding to the absence of pain and “10” to the maximal imaginable pain) presented horizontally to the patient. 34 Patients were asked to rate minimal pain intensity, maximal pain intensity and average pain intensity experienced during the foregoing week. Statistical analysis The a-priori power analysis was based on testing if the mean change of the CMS total score of the intervention group was significantly greater than the mean change of CMS total score of the control group. As the CMS was expected to have a non-normal distribution, a nonparametric test (Mann-Whitney) was used with an α of 0.05, power of 80%. This calculation revealed that a sample of 15 patients in each group was required to be able to reject the null hypothesis. Descriptive statistics were calculated. Frequencies of demographic and clinical variables were compared between groups (intervention vs . control) using χ 2 tests. Univariate ANOVA was used to compare baseline variables between groups (intervention vs . control). Repeated ANOVA with between-subjects factor GROUP (intervention vs . control) and within-subjects factor TIME (T0, T1, T2, T3) was used to assess interactions for primary and secondary outcome variables (VAS pain, CMS total score). Data were expressed as means (±1SD) or medians (25 th percentile – 75 th percentile). In all cases, P<0.050 was regarded as significant. All statistical analyses were carried out with SPSS Statistics 27.0 package (IBM ® SPSS ® Statistics, 2020; IBM Corp., Armonk, NY, USA). Results Participants Thirty-five patients were included in this study (intervention: N.=19; control: N.=16). All patients completed the study. Demographic and clinical characteristics of patients were similar between the study groups at baseline T0 ( Table I ). Table I. —Demographical characteristics and baseline clinical assessments of patients with subacute adhesive capsulitis (intervention vs . control) (N.=35). Variables Control group (N.=16) Ropivacaine group (N.=19) Significance P Demographical/clinical characteristics Gender (male/female) 8/8 3/16 0.065 Age (yrs, mean±S.D.) 55±11.9 54±6.4 0.909 Shoulder (R/L) 5 R/11 L 12 R/7 L 0.092 Dominant shoulder (yes, N., % of group) 5 (31.3%) 12 (57.9%) 0.176 Duration symptoms (weeks, mean±S.D.) 15±4.9 13±4.0 0.282 Diabetes (yes, N., % of group) 5 (31.3%) 2 (10.5%) 0.207 Painkillers (yes, N., % of group) 11 (68.8%) 14 (73.4%) 0.424 Physiotherapy (yes, N., % of group) 10 (62.5%) 13 (68.4%) 0.736 Assessments at baseline (T0) Constant-Murley Score (total score) 26.1±6.43 23.0±8.65 0.101 ROM anterior elevation (°, mean ±S.D.) 84.8±4.93 82.7±4.93 0.785 ROM abduction (°, mean±S.D.) 64.7±6.35 67.8±4.93 0.614 ROM external rotation (median; CI 95%) 4.0; 2.72-7.16 2.0; 1.18-4.52 0.126 ROM internal rotation (median; CI 95%) 4.0; 2.24-5.64 2.0; 1.69-3.37 0.107 Pain intensity (Visual Analogue Scale 0 –10) 5.8 SD 1.61 6.3 SD 1.36 0.410 Side-effects of injection Number of patients with side-effects at any time point (yes, N., % of group) 2 (12.5%) 9 (47.37%) 0.035* Open in a new tab Univariate ANOVA was used to examine interactions between groups for parametric variables. χ 2 was used to examine frequency differences between groups (intervention vs . control). *P<0.050 Patients in the intervention group more frequently had side effects from the injection, compared to patients in the control group (intervention: 45.8% vs. control: 14.3%, χ 2 : P=0.035). The results of the assessments at T0, T1, T2 and T3 for the primary and secondary outcome variables are shown in Figure 1 . Figure 1. Open in a new tab —Outcome variables of the randomized trial comparing ropivacaine suprascapular nerve blocks versus saline injections for treating subacute adhesive capsulitis: before the first injection (T0), before the second injection (T1), before the third injection (T2), and one month after the third injection (T3). Injections at T0, T1 and T2 were scheduled at 1-week intervals. Outcomes are shown separately for patients in the intervention group (solid line) and in the control group (dotted line). No significant differences were observed between the intervention group and the control group with regards to shoulder function (Constant-Murley Score), range of motion and pain intensity. Constant-Murley Score Repeated ANOVA with between-subjects factor GROUP (intervention vs . control) and within-subjects factor TIME (T0, T1, T2, T3) was used to assess changes of the Constant-Murley Score during the study ( Figure 1A ). For the total Constant-Murley Score, no significant effects were observed for the factor GROUP (F 1,33 =0.115, P=0.736) or for the interaction between GROUPxTIME (F 3,33 =0.431, P=0.732). A significant of effect of the factor TIME was observed for the total score (F 3,33 =21.349; P<0.001). 75% of patients reached the MCID for CMS score in the intervention group and 58% of patients in the control group (Chi-square: P=0.476). Pain assessment Repeated ANOVA with between-subjects factor GROUP (intervention vs . control) and within-subjects factor TIME (T0, T1, T2, T3) was used to assess changes of pain intensity during the study ( Figure 1B ). For the average pain intensity, no significant effect was observed for the factor GROUP (F 1,33 =0.008, P=0.930) or for the interaction between GROUPxTIME (F 3,33 =0.795, P=0.500). A significant of effect of the factor TIME was observed for average pain intensity (F 3,33 =11.189; P<0.001). Range of motion of the shoulder Repeated ANOVA with between-subjects factor GROUP (intervention vs . control) and within-subjects factor TIME (T0, T1, T2, T3) was used to assess for changes of ROM during the study ( Figure 1C-F ). For all ROM variables (anterior elevation, abduction, external rotation, internal rotation), no significant effects were observed for the factor GROUP (all P>0.162) or for the interaction between GROUP×TIME (all P>0.385). A significant effect of the factor TIME was observed for anterior elevation (F 3,33 =6.565; P<0.001), abduction (F 3,33 =13.861; P<0.001), external rotation (F 3,33 =9.901; P<0.001) and internal rotation (F 3,33 =7.313; P=0.011). Discussion The aim of this study was to examine the effectiveness of three successive ropivacaine ultrasound-guided SSNB at one-week intervals ( vs . control) in subacute adhesive capsulitis. Results showed no significant differences between the intervention group and the control group with regards to shoulder function (Constant-Murley Score), range of motion and pain intensity. Though ropivacaine ultrasound guided nerve blocks of the suprascapular nerve did not provide any benefit over saline injections, all outcome variables improved significantly over time in both groups. Interestingly, most patients in both groups reached the minimal clinical important difference threshold for the Constant-Murley Score over the duration of the study protocol. The lack of superiority of ropivacaine SSNB over saline for treating adhesive capsulitis in the present study was unexpected since previous publications consistently showed that anesthetic blocks of the suprascapular nerve elicited better outcomes on shoulder function and pain. 8, 9, 13, 16, 17, 19, 21, 23 In general, negative findings elicit suspicion upon the adequacy of the sample size and a possible lack of statistical power. 35 However, the present sample was adequately powered for the researched outcomes and the sample size was comparable to that of other randomized trials reporting suprascapular nerve block’s efficacy. The present negative findings warrant some considerations on the natural history of adhesive capsulitis, on the patient selection for trials, on the type of drug used for injection and on placebo effect of injections. First, adhesive capsulitis is a self-limiting condition that is characterized by pain and stiffness in the shoulder progressing through different stages. 1, 2, 25 In general, pain and limitation of range of motion peak at 3 to 6 months after the onset of symptoms, followed by a spontaneous progressive decrease of pain. 1, 7 Previous studies on nerve blocks either did not report on the mean duration of symptoms before enrolment 10, 12, 21 or mainly recruited patients in the chronic phase (>6 months). 8, 9, 13, 15-18 It is possible that SSNBs are less effective in subacute adhesive capsulitis (disease duration: 14 weeks ± 4.5 in the present study) than in chronic stages. It could be hypothesized that acute and subacute stages of adhesive capsulitis are linked with inflammatory tissue changes 36-38 that do not respond as well to anesthetic blocks as to glucocorticoid injections. To our knowledge, the present study is the first to specifically address the analgesic effect of SSNB in the most painful months of adhesive capsulitis. It appears that SSNBs as a stand-alone treatment are not sufficient to induce significant changes in shoulder function, pain and mobility compared to placebo at the subacute stage in contrast with previous results at the chronic stage. 9 Consequently, the conjunction of glucocorticoid injection and SSNBs may be a more interesting strategy for treating the subacute phase of adhesive capsulitis than SSNB blocks alone. 12, 13, 17, 19, 22, 23 Second, beneficial effects of SSNB were reported from studies that had a different methodological design than the present study. Most previous studies did not include a control group, 10, 12, 13 were retrospective 13 or not double blinded. 8, 15-17 The stronger methodological quality of the present randomized-controlled, double-blinded study allows for a more accurate interpretation of the clinical improvement seen in patients with adhesive capsulitis. Indeed, patients in the intervention group showed a significant improvement over time, which was similar to that seen in the control group. It is impossible to distinguish a strong placebo effect (in both groups) from a lack of effectiveness of the treatment intervention. Recent research on the placebo effects of pain therapies indicates that more than 50% of treatment effects can be attributed to a placebo effect. In double-blind studies designed to investigate placebo effects on pain, the responses to placebo were similar to the responses to active treatment. 39, 40 In particular, response to local anesthetic injections of zygoapophysary joints in chronic low back pain demonstrated a very large response to placebo injections, both in response rate (78%) and magnitude (effect size d=1.85). 41 Similarly, it is known that the placebo effect in knee injections for osteoarthritis carries a long-term effect (stable up to 6 months) on functioning and pain. 42, 43 These findings emphasize the need for randomized-controlled trials considering possible placebo effects when investigating injections for the treatment of patients with adhesive capsulitis. Third, it could be argued that the volume of local anesthetic injected in the present study (5 mL) was smaller than in most published studies (10 mL), 8, 9, 13, 15-17, 19 and that this may have precluded a good treatment response. Nonetheless, research by Feigl et al . has shown that a volume of 5 mL is sufficient to fill the supraspinous fossa and reach the suprascapular nerve with a lower risk of extension of the local anesthetic to the axillary fossa compared to a 10 mL injection. 44 Another technical consideration concerns the duration of action of the local anesthetic. A sensory block elicited with ropivacaine has been shown to last from 9.2 hours to 17.3 hours in healthy volunteers (peroneal nerve block) depending on the concentration used. 45-47 Though ropivacaine is considered as a long-acting anesthetic product, its duration of action is not sufficient to bridge between successive weekly injections and up to one-month follow-up. It has been suggested that local anesthetic blocks may induce prolonged analgesic effects through subsequent changes in the central nervous system. 11, 13, 15, 16, 19, 48 Clearly, no such prolonged effects were observed in the present study. Though a longer follow-up time could be of clinical interest given the long-time duration of disease, it is not expected that a short-lived anesthetic block could induce changes in the long-term if no changes are observed in the short-term. If the volume and product injected for the SSNBs were sound, then one could ask oneself if the product was injected in the correct place. All SSNB and saline injections in this study were realized with ultrasound guidance. According to Gorthi et al ., 49 the analgesic effect of ultrasound guided SSNB is better sustained at 1 month compared to anatomical landmark injection. Many authors conclude that ultrasound-guidance is the method of choice for SSNBs. 23, 29, 30, 49, 50 An indirect clue that the implemented technical procedure was adequate, is that the frequency of side effects typically seen with local anesthesia was higher in the intervention group than the control group. We must acknowledge that to avoid unblinding, no systematic clinical assessments to monitor local anesthesia immediately after injection were performed during this study protocol, in contrast with other studies. 15, 23 Finally, this trial investigated the usefulness of a suprascapular nerve block as a stand-alone treatment for adhesive capsulitis intervention. One could argue that a nerve block is more effective when combined with a specific rehabilitation program. To our knowledge, the latest evidence concerning the use of manual therapy and exercise for frozen shoulder (Cochrane review 2014) concluded that these approaches yielded uncertain results and that high-quality RCTs are needed to establish their benefits and harms. 51 Thus, a specific rehabilitation intervention was not included as a treatment strategy in the present research protocol. Instead, patients were advised to carry on with their on-going rehabilitation treatment without any changes during the duration of this trial. Furthermore, the percentage of patients benefiting from on-going physiotherapy was similar in the intervention group and the placebo group ( Table I ). Conclusions In conclusion, suprascapular nerve blocks alone do not perform better than saline for treating the subacute stage of adhesive capsulitis up to one month after 3 successive injections. Many patients in this study reached a clinically significant improvement of shoulder function after a 4-week follow-up regardless of the treatment arm. Given that SSNBs elicited more frequently side-effects (without added effectiveness) compared to saline, the place of SSNBs in the treatment strategy of adhesive capsulitis throughout its different stages, needs to be rediscussed. Future studies also should explore the combination of SSNB and glucocorticoid injections for the subacute phase of adhesive capsulitis. References 1. Neviaser AS, Hannafin JA. Adhesive capsulitis: a review of current treatment. Am J Sports Med 2010;38:2346–56. https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/query.fcgi?cmd=Retrieve&db=PubMed&list_uids=20110457&dopt=Abstract 10.1177/0363546509348048 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Hand C, Clipsham K, Rees JL, Carr AJ. Long-term outcome of frozen shoulder. J Shoulder Elbow Surg 2008;17:231–6. https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/query.fcgi?cmd=Retrieve&db=PubMed&list_uids=17993282&dopt=Abstract 10.1016/j.jse.2007.05.009 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Schiefer M, Teixeira PF, Fontenelle C, Carminatti T, Santos DA, Righi LD, et al. Prevalence of hypothyroidism in patients with frozen shoulder. J Shoulder Elbow Surg 2017;26:49–55. https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/query.fcgi?cmd=Retrieve&db=PubMed&list_uids=27424251&dopt=Abstract 10.1016/j.jse.2016.04.026 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Smith SP, Devaraj VS, Bunker TD. The association between frozen shoulder and Dupuytren’s disease. J Shoulder Elbow Surg 2001;10:149–51. https://www.ncbi.